# Communication Hub: every API, step by step
#
# Run top to bottom: health → tenant + API key → providers → templates → devices → send →
# track → inbox → clean up. 2.0a / 2.0b add console providers (logged, not sent) for a safe demo.
#
# Open in VS Code with the "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client): click "Send Request"
# above each request, top to bottom. Ids and the API key are taken from earlier responses
# automatically. Replace <...> values in bodies with your own (passwords, tokens).
# {{name}}-style placeholders inside template bodies are Handlebars for the hub: REST Client
# sends them as they are (it may underline them as unknown variables; that is fine).
# No REST Client? Copy the method, URL, headers and JSON body into Swagger/Postman.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000/api
@opsUrl = http://localhost:9464
# ADMIN_API_KEY from your .env
@ADMIN_KEY = paste-your-ADMIN_API_KEY-here
# FCM registration token from your app (push / in-app only)
@FCM_TOKEN = paste-a-device-token-here


### ===== STEP 0: Check the service  ·  anyone =====

### 0.1 · Liveness
# Ops server answers directly (no DB). Use it to check the process is up.
# Expected: 200
# @name r1_liveness
GET {{opsUrl}}/health/live

### 0.2 · Readiness
# Ops server pings Postgres and Redis. 200 = ready, 503 = a dependency is down.
# Expected: 200
# @name r2_readiness
GET {{opsUrl}}/health/ready

### 0.3 · Prometheus metrics
# Prometheus metrics (requests, queue depth, deliveries per provider). Scraped by Prometheus, shown in
# Grafana.
# Expected: 200
# @name r3_prometheus_metrics
GET {{opsUrl}}/metrics

### 0.4 · gRPC health check
# Standard gRPC health check on :50051.
# @name r4_grpc_health_check
gRPC grpcurl -plaintext localhost:50051 grpc.health.v1.Health/Check

{ "service": "" }


### ===== STEP 1: Create the tenant and its API key  ·  Platform admin =====

### 1.1 · Create tenant
# Admin key checked → tenant row created → starter templates (otp, welcome) copied → first API key
# generated (only its hash is stored) → key returned ONCE.
# Expected: 201
# @name r5_create_tenant
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants
Content-Type: application/json
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

{
  "name": "Acme Inc.",
  "slug": "acme",
  "settings": { "appName": "Acme" }
}

### 1.2 · List tenants
# Admin key checked → all tenants from DB.
# Expected: 200
# @name r6_list_tenants
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

### 1.3 · Get tenant
# Admin key checked → one tenant by id.
# Path params: tenant id = <TENANT_ID>  (tenant.id from Create tenant)
# Expected: 200
# @name r7_get_tenant
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants/{{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.tenant.id}}
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

### 1.4 · Update tenant (rename, rebrand, deactivate)
# Admin key checked → given fields updated, settings merged → tenant cache cleared. active=false
# blocks every key of the tenant.
# Path params: tenant id = <TENANT_ID>  (tenant.id from Create tenant)
# Expected: 200
# @name r8_update_tenant_rename_rebrand_deactiva
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants/{{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.tenant.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

{
  "name": "Acme Corporation",
  "settings": { "appName": "Acme Corp" },
  "active": true
}

### 1.5 · Create another API key
# Admin key checked → new key generated, hash stored → key returned ONCE.
# Path params: tenant id = <TENANT_ID>  (tenant.id from Create tenant)
# Expected: 201
# @name r9_create_another_api_key
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants/{{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.tenant.id}}/api-keys
Content-Type: application/json
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

{
  "name": "backend-prod",
  "expiresAt": "2027-12-31T00:00:00Z"
}

### 1.6 · List API keys
# Admin key checked → the tenant's keys (prefix only, never the key).
# Path params: tenant id = <TENANT_ID>  (tenant.id from Create tenant)
# Expected: 200
# @name r10_list_api_keys
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants/{{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.tenant.id}}/api-keys
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}


### ===== STEP 2: Add providers and From addresses  ·  Tenant =====

### 2.0a · Create demo email provider (console)
# API key → tenant → config validated (vendor rules, From names) → saved in provider_configs
# (credentials encrypted) → Redis cache providers:<tenant>:CHANNEL_EMAIL refreshed → next email uses
# it.
# Expected: 201
# @name r11_create_demo_email_provider_console
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "provider": "console",
  "name": "demo-email",
  "priority": 1,
  "settings": { "from": "Acme <no-reply@acme.com>" },
  "fromAddresses": {
    "billing": "Acme Billing <billing@acme.com>",
    "support": "Acme Support <support@acme.com>"
  },
  "limits": { "rateMax": 20, "rateDurationMs": 1000, "maxAttempts": 3, "backoffMs": 2000 }
}

### 2.0b · Create demo SMS provider (console)
# Same as 2.0a for CHANNEL_SMS.
# Expected: 201
# @name r12_create_demo_sms_provider_console
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_SMS",
  "provider": "console",
  "name": "demo-sms",
  "priority": 1,
  "settings": { "from": "+14155550100" },
  "fromAddresses": { "india": "ACMEIN" }
}

### 2.1 · Create provider
# Same as 2.0a with a real SMTP account: credentials encrypted, cache refreshed, used by the next
# email.
# Expected: 201
# @name r13_create_provider
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "provider": "smtp",
  "name": "smtp-main",
  "priority": 1,
  "settings": { "host": "smtp.acme.com", "port": "587", "secure": "false", "from": "Acme <no-reply@acme.com>" },
  "credentials": { "user": "mailer@acme.com", "pass": "<SMTP_PASSWORD>" },
  "fromAddresses": {
    "support": "Acme Support <support@acme.com>",
    "billing": "Acme Billing <billing@acme.com>"
  },
  "limits": { "rateMax": 20, "rateDurationMs": 1000, "maxAttempts": 3, "backoffMs": 2000 }
}

### 2.2 · List providers
# Tenant's providers from Redis/DB, in failover (priority) order. Credentials never returned, only
# their names.
# Query params: channel = CHANNEL_EMAIL
# Expected: 200
# @name r14_list_providers
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers?channel=CHANNEL_EMAIL
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 2.3 · Get provider
# One provider by id.
# Path params: provider id = <PROVIDER_ID>  (id from Create provider)
# Expected: 200
# @name r15_get_provider
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers/{{r13_create_provider.response.body.$.id}}
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 2.4 · Update provider
# Validated → merged into the stored config → Redis cache refreshed → applies to the next message (no
# restart).
# Path params: provider id = <PROVIDER_ID>  (id from Create provider)
# Expected: 200
# @name r16_update_provider
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers/{{r13_create_provider.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "priority": 5,
  "active": true,
  "settings": { "from": "Acme <hello@acme.com>" },
  "fromAddresses": { "news": "Acme News <news@acme.com>", "alerts": "" },
  "credentials": { "user": "mailer@acme.com", "pass": "<NEW_PASSWORD>" },
  "limits": { "maxConcurrency": 0, "maxAttempts": 5 }
}

### 2.6 · List From addresses
# Every From (default + named) of every active email/SMS provider, with the provider that owns it.
# Pick a name here for "from" when sending.
# Query params: channel = CHANNEL_EMAIL
# Expected: 200
# @name r17_list_from_addresses
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/from-addresses?channel=CHANNEL_EMAIL
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}


### ===== STEP 3: Templates  ·  Tenant =====

### 3.1 · Create template
# Handlebars compiled to check it → template + version 1 saved and activated.
# Expected: 201
# @name r18_create_template
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "name": "invoice-ready",
  "description": "Invoice notification",
  "content": {
    "subject": "Invoice {{invoiceNo}} from {{appName}}",
    "html": "<p>Hi {{name}}, your invoice <b>{{invoiceNo}}</b> of {{amount}} is ready.</p>",
    "body": "Hi {{name}}, your invoice {{invoiceNo}} of {{amount}} is ready."
  }
}

### 3.2 · List templates
# The tenant's templates (starters included), paginated.
# Query params: channel = CHANNEL_EMAIL; page.limit = 20
# Expected: 200
# @name r19_list_templates
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates?channel=CHANNEL_EMAIL&page.limit=20
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 3.3 · Get template
# One template with its active and latest version numbers.
# Path params: template id = <TEMPLATE_ID>  (id from Create template)
# Expected: 200
# @name r20_get_template
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates/{{r18_create_template.response.body.$.id}}
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 3.4 · Publish a new version
# New version compiled and saved; activate=true makes it the one new sends use. Already queued
# messages keep their version.
# Path params: template id = <TEMPLATE_ID>  (id from Create template)
# Expected: 201
# @name r21_publish_a_new_version
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates/{{r18_create_template.response.body.$.id}}/versions
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "content": {
    "subject": "Your {{appName}} invoice {{invoiceNo}}",
    "html": "<p>Hello {{name}}, invoice <b>{{invoiceNo}}</b> ({{amount}}) is ready.</p>"
  },
  "activate": true
}

### 3.5 · List versions
# All versions, newest first; active=true marks the live one.
# Path params: template id = <TEMPLATE_ID>  (id from Create template)
# Expected: 200
# @name r22_list_versions
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates/{{r18_create_template.response.body.$.id}}/versions
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 3.6 · Activate a version (publish / roll back)
# Switches the active version (roll back or publish a staged one).
# Path params: template id = <TEMPLATE_ID>  (id from Create template); version = 1
# Expected: 200
# @name r23_activate_a_version_publish_roll_back
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates/{{r18_create_template.response.body.$.id}}/versions/1/activate
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 3.7 · Preview
# Renders the version with your data. Nothing is stored or sent.
# Path params: template id = <TEMPLATE_ID>  (id from Create template)
# Expected: 200
# @name r24_preview
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/templates/{{r18_create_template.response.body.$.id}}/preview
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "version": 2,
  "data": { "name": "Jane", "invoiceNo": "INV-1042", "amount": "$120" }
}


### ===== STEP 4: Register devices (push and in-app)  ·  Tenant backend =====

### 4.1 · Register device
# Stores the FCM token for the user (upsert). Push and in-app sends with userId go to all the user's
# active tokens.
# Expected: 200
# @name r25_register_device
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/devices
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "userId": "user-42",
  "token": "{{FCM_TOKEN}}",
  "platform": "DEVICE_PLATFORM_ANDROID"
}

### 4.2 · List a user's devices
# The user's registered devices.
# Query params: user_id = user-42
# Expected: 200
# @name r26_list_a_user_s_devices
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/devices?user_id=user-42
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}


### ===== STEP 5: Send notifications  ·  Tenant backend =====

### 5.1 · Send (email with template)
# Validate → template active version pinned → "from" name resolved to the providers that have it →
# saved (recipient + data encrypted) as QUEUED → job on notifications-email → 202 with queue state.
# Worker: render → provider (priority order, rate/concurrency check in Redis) → SENT or
# retry/failover.
# Expected: 202
# @name r27_send_email_with_template
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "template": "invoice-ready",
  "data": { "name": "Jane", "invoiceNo": "INV-1042", "amount": "$120" },
  "from": "billing",
  "idempotencyKey": "invoice-INV-1042"
}

### 5.2 · Send (email without template)
# Same as 5.1 without a template: subject/body sent as given ({{placeholders}} still filled from
# data). "from" as a full address.
# Expected: 202
# @name r28_send_email_without_template
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "subject": "Your receipt",
  "body": "Thanks for your payment, {{name}}.",
  "data": { "name": "Jane" },
  "from": "Acme Billing <billing@acme.com>"
}

### 5.3 · Send (SMS OTP, high priority)
# Same flow on notifications-sms with priority 1 (high) so OTPs go before everything else. "india" →
# only SMS providers with that From name.
# Expected: 202
# @name r29_send_sms_otp_high_priority
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_SMS",
  "recipient": "+919876543210",
  "template": "otp",
  "data": { "code": "482913", "expiresInMinutes": 5 },
  "priority": "PRIORITY_HIGH",
  "from": "india",
  "idempotencyKey": "otp-user-42-1730000000"
}

### 5.4 · Send (push to all of a user's devices)
# Saved → job on notifications-push → worker loads all active device tokens of userId → FCM (tenant's
# fcm provider). Dead tokens are deactivated.
# Expected: 202
# @name r30_send_push_to_all_of_a_user_s_devices
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_PUSH",
  "userId": "user-42",
  "subject": "Order shipped",
  "body": "Order {{orderId}} is on its way",
  "data": { "orderId": "ORD-77" },
  "metadata": { "deeplink": "app://orders/77" }
}

### 5.5 · Send (in-app inbox + push)
# Saved → worker writes the message to the user's inbox → FCM signal to the devices (visible push
# because alsoPush=true, else silent data message type=inbox).
# Expected: 202
# @name r31_send_in_app_inbox_push
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_IN_APP",
  "userId": "user-42",
  "subject": "Invoice ready",
  "body": "Invoice {{invoiceNo}} is ready",
  "data": { "invoiceNo": "INV-1042" },
  "metadata": { "deeplink": "app://invoices/1042" },
  "alsoPush": true
}

### 5.6 · Send (scheduled)
# Saved as QUEUED → delayed job that runs at scheduledAt. Can be cancelled until then (6.4).
# Expected: 202
# @name r32_send_scheduled
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "template": "welcome",
  "data": { "name": "Jane" },
  "scheduledAt": "2026-11-20T09:00:00Z"
}

### 5.7 · Send bulk (campaigns, up to 500)
# Each item validated and saved → jobs added with low priority (10) so they never delay single sends.
# Expected: 202
# @name r33_send_bulk_campaigns_up_to_500
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/bulk
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "notifications": [
    { "channel": "CHANNEL_SMS", "recipient": "+14155550101", "body": "Sale starts now!" },
    { "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL", "recipient": "bob@example.com", "subject": "Sale", "body": "Sale starts now!", "from": "support" },
    { "channel": "CHANNEL_PUSH", "userId": "user-42", "subject": "Sale", "body": "Sale starts now!" }
  ]
}

### 5.8 · Send bulk: same message to many recipients
# One item, many targets. Email / SMS / push tokens: "recipients": [...] or a comma-separated
# "recipient". Push / in-app users: "userIds": [...]. Each target becomes its own notification (own
# id, status, retry), in order, duplicates dropped; accepted = number of targets. idempotencyKey gets
# a per-recipient suffix ("key:<hash>") whenever the list form is used, even with one entry, so
# repeating the request or adding recipients never re-sends to earlier ones. At most 500 targets
# listed in total (duplicates included). Several recipients and several userIds in one item is a
# VALIDATION_ERROR; recipients / userIds on POST /v1/notifications (single send) is a
# VALIDATION_ERROR.
# Expected: 202
# @name r34_send_bulk_same_message_to_many_recip
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/bulk
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "notifications": [
    {
      "channel": "CHANNEL_EMAIL",
      "recipients": ["jane@example.com", "bob@example.com"],
      "template": "invoice-ready",
      "data": { "name": "Jane", "invoiceNo": "INV-1042", "amount": "$120" },
      "from": "billing",
      "idempotencyKey": "invoice-ready-INV-1042"
    },
    {
      "channel": "CHANNEL_SMS",
      "recipient": "+919876543210, +919876543211",
      "template": "otp",
      "data": { "code": "482913", "expiresInMinutes": 5 },
      "from": "india"
    },
    {
      "channel": "CHANNEL_PUSH",
      "userIds": ["user-42", "user-43"],
      "subject": "Sale",
      "body": "Sale starts now!"
    }
  ]
}


### ===== STEP 6: Track, retry and cancel  ·  Tenant backend =====

### 6.1 · Get notification
# Notification + every provider attempt (which provider, which From, error, duration) + live BullMQ
# job state.
# Path params: notification id = <NOTIFICATION_ID>  (notification.id from a send)
# Expected: 200
# @name r35_get_notification
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/{{r27_send_email_with_template.response.body.$.notification.id}}
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 6.2 · List notifications
# Tenant's notifications, newest first, filtered. recipient filter uses a keyed hash (data is
# encrypted).
# Query params: status = NOTIFICATION_STATUS_FAILED; channel = CHANNEL_EMAIL; page.limit = 20
# Expected: 200
# @name r36_list_notifications
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications?status=NOTIFICATION_STATUS_FAILED&channel=CHANNEL_EMAIL&page.limit=20
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 6.3 · Retry a failed notification
# FAILED → QUEUED, new job; renders the same template version again.
# Path params: notification id = <FAILED_ID>  (id of a FAILED notification (List notifications))
# Expected: 200
# @name r37_retry_a_failed_notification
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/{{r36_list_notifications.response.body.$.notifications[0].id}}/retry
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 6.4 · Cancel a queued / scheduled notification
# QUEUED/RETRYING → CANCELLED; the job is removed from the queue.
# Path params: notification id = <SCHEDULED_ID>  (notification.id from the scheduled send)
# Expected: 200
# @name r38_cancel_a_queued_scheduled_notificati
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/notifications/{{r32_send_scheduled.response.body.$.notification.id}}/cancel
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}


### ===== STEP 7: In-app inbox  ·  Tenant backend, for the signed-in user =====

### 7.1 · List inbox messages
# The user's inbox messages (written by in-app sends) + unread count.
# Path params: user_id = user-42  (your user id)
# Query params: unread_only = true; page.limit = 20
# Expected: 200
# @name r39_list_inbox_messages
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/users/user-42/inbox?unread_only=true&page.limit=20
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 7.2 · Unread count (badge)
# Unread count only (for the app badge).
# Path params: user_id = user-42  (your user id)
# Expected: 200
# @name r40_unread_count_badge
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/users/user-42/inbox/unread-count
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 7.3 · Mark messages read
# Marks the given messages read → new unread count.
# Path params: user_id = user-42  (your user id)
# Expected: 200
# @name r41_mark_messages_read
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/users/user-42/inbox/read
Content-Type: application/json
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

{
  "ids": ["{{r39_list_inbox_messages.response.body.$.messages[0].id}}"]
}

### 7.4 · Mark all read
# Marks all read → 0.
# Path params: user_id = user-42  (your user id)
# Expected: 200
# @name r42_mark_all_read
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/users/user-42/inbox/read-all
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}

### 7.5 · Delete inbox message
# Deletes one message → new unread count.
# Path params: user_id = user-42  (your user id); inbox id = <INBOX_MESSAGE_ID>  (messages[0].id from List inbox messages)
# Expected: 200
# @name r43_delete_inbox_message
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/users/user-42/inbox/{{r39_list_inbox_messages.response.body.$.messages[0].id}}
x-api-key: {{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.apiKey.key}}


### ===== STEP 8: Clean up (destructive: run last)  ·  Admin / Tenant =====

### 8.1 · Revoke API key
# Key revoked immediately; calls with it get 401.
# Path params: tenant id = <TENANT_ID>  (tenant.id from Create tenant); api-key id = <KEY_ID>  (apiKey.id from Create API key)
# Expected: 204
# @name r44_revoke_api_key
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/tenants/{{r5_create_tenant.response.body.$.tenant.id}}/api-keys/{{r9_create_another_api_key.response.body.$.apiKey.id}}
x-admin-key: {{ADMIN_KEY}}

### 8.2 · Delete provider
# Provider deleted → Redis cache refreshed → the next message uses the remaining providers (or the
# platform default).
# Path params: provider id = <PROVIDER_ID>  (id from Create provider)
# Expected: 204
# @name r45_delete_provider
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/providers/{{r13_create_provider.response.body.$.id}}
